1. Upstream drains data events via the onData listener (which timestamps); the port drains channel events (including Resize). A resize flood could extend the drain. Minor.
2. Upstream timestamps on every data arrival; port drains queued events at the top of each loop iteration. Between sleeps, arriving events are timestamped at the NEXT loop iteration — at most one sleep duration late. Upstream timestamps immediately (synchronously at event arrival). So the port's idle detection is delayed by up to `idle` (50ms): an event arriving right after `last_data_time` check → port sleeps min(time_left, idle); wakes; updates last_data_time. Upstream: event sets lastDataTime immediately, then the pending sleep completes, then the loop rechecks: now - lastDataTime >= idle? The event arrived during the sleep, so elapsed since event < idle → continues draining. Same outcome — the port effectively sees the same. OK.
